   summary:"Chávez grew up poor, one of ten children living in a converted railway carriage, and said he boxed so his mother would never have to take in washing and ironing again. After a 13-bout amateur career he turned professional at 17 and went unbeaten through his first 90 fights, 89 wins and a draw, winning world titles at super featherweight in 1984, lightweight in 1987 and light welterweight in 1989. The Hall of Fame credits a great chin, a relentless, aggressive style and a vicious body attack for making him a national hero. His 1993 win over Greg Haugen at the Estadio Azteca drew 132,274 people, still the biggest crowd for a championship fight.",
